00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.910
- [Wenhu] Yeah, let's
review some of the basics.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.880
So neural net, I guess
all of you already know

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.010
like how neural nets are working.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.490
So neural nets consists
of like a lot of neurons,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.370
and also synapsis.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.090
Basically the weights
connecting different neurons,

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.190
these are the learnable parameters.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.620
We're going to optimize these parameters

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.480
to make this neural net

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.410
to accomplish any task that we desire.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.330
So in computer vision,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.880
there is convolutional neural net,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.360
which basically takes images as the input

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.990
and then try to run some
small convolutional kernels

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.500
to extract localized
features from the image,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.600
and then do some downstream task.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.020
Like detections, image classifications,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.403
and so on and so forth.

00:03:08.609 --> 00:03:13.609
And so in NLP, we normally,
we talk about a lot of tasks

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.030
like machine translation, summarization,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.060
image captioning, so on and so forth.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.410
A lot of this NLP task can
be unified into one format

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:29.220
which is basically a sequence
of items as the input,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.240
and then a sequence of
items as the output.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.620
So the items could be a lot of things.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.950
This items in the sequence
could be like words,

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.510
letters, image features
and so on and so forth.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.630
That's why like a predominant,

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.270
like a view of NLP is basically called

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.013
sequence-to-sequence modeling.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.640
So I'm not sure if you can see the video.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.100
Yeah, it's running well.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.020
And so sequence-to-sequence model

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.600
basically takes a sequence,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.940
like item by item as the
input and try to output

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:11.103
a lot of items like also token by token.

00:04:15.843 --> 00:04:17.730
And then the sequence-to-sequence model.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.160
So one of the most popular architecture

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.090
is called encoder and decoder model.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.560
So it basically, the encoder
will compile the information

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.620
it receives into a vector.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.710
And then after processing
this information,

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.130
this vector will be sent to the decoder,

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.760
and then the decoder will
start generating things.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.733
Like tokens or paragraphs or anything.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.530
So this is also like
kind of a visualization,

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.470
so this is a machine translation task.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:56.550
You can think of it as a
sequence-to-sequence modeling.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.080
So on the left hand side

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.240
you have like maybe some French words,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.470
you kind of tokenize them
into individual tokens

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.740
and then you'll feed this
tokens one by one to an encoder,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.770
and then the encoder try
to comprehend the meeting

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.220
and then compress all the
information right into a vector.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.430
And then this vector will be
passed to the decoder model

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.280
to start generating maybe
like the translation

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.853
of the French sentence,
something like that.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:27.660
So how does it work?

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.270
So how does the encoder actually work?

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.790
What's the underlying mechanism?

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:37.020
So the very basic one is
called recurrent neural net.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:37.980
Recurrent neural net,

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:42.510
which is basically a
recurrent computation machine.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.910
So you can think of it as

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:48.540
like several components
here you have the input,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.050
which is X, where you have also a state,

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.700
this is the most important part.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.000
So the state will receive input of X

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.980
and then try to update its belief

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.950
about what it has perceived so far,

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:06.003
and then it tries to
generate some output as all.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:11.580
And so this recurrence can be
unfolded from left to right

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.400
into this kind of mechanism.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.690
So it's basically some sort
of multi-layer perception,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.150
but it's kind of enrolled in

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.760
to include the time dimension as well.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.220
And a lot of this weights are shared

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.873
across different timestamp.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.650
So this is a recurrent recursion formula,

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.900
which is pretty simple, right?

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.680
You take the input and then
multiply with the matrix,

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.840
and then the previous state also multiply

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.610
with the transition matrix,

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.033
and then you kind of update
the state from ST-1 to ST.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.050
And then you output the,

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.240
like the output state of OT

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.343
by multiply another matrix over ST.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:58.323
So this is the most basic
recurrent neural network.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:03.660
And then one of the drawback
of this simple design

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:06.000
is that it's totally linear, right?

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.490
I'd say it's not able to capture

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.670
a lot of this very complex behavior

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.200
in our natural language

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.590
or any other sequence to sequence task.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.320
That's why people propose like different,

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.110
more advanced recurrent neural nets.

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:26.610
Like one example is this long
short-term memory network,

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.590
and there are also some other variants

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.573
like AGIU or something like that.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:35.010
So basically in this type of model,

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:39.150
so it includes more like
non-linearity basically,

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.550
there are some activation function,

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.290
basically it's composed of
like sigmoid or hypertension

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:49.680
that kind of function so that the model

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.530
or the architecture can be more expressive

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:56.280
in terms of modeling
different types of behavior.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.060
And the key findings of
this architecture is that

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.660
it has three different mechanism.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.910
One is the forget gate,

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.560
the other is the input gate.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.050
And finally they have like a output gate,

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.230
which is basically the FT for gap gate,

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.690
I is the input gate and O, right?

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.660
So we can see there's
a lot of stuff going on

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.270
in this one transition function.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.543
So let's break this down
into several different part.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.880
And for example, if we want
to comprehend the sentence,

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.320
Bob is a nice person

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.470
and then on the other hand is evil, right?

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:36.660
So at the sentence break,

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.820
when you start a new sentence,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:42.750
we do want to forget about
like Bob or whatsoever

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.180
and then start a new sentence, right?

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.960
So that's why the LSTM
introduced this mechanism

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.390
of forgetting gate, right?

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.730
So at some step,

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:56.700
so the model tries to predict

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.760
like a number between zero and one,

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.340
which means that how
much memory should I keep

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:03.690
from the last stage?

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:05.310
If this is like a new sentence,

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:08.070
probably I just throw
away all the memories

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:11.910
about like the previous context
and start from beginning.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.070
If I'm in the middle of the sentence

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:17.610
I should probably have like a higher value

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.800
of like retaining the past context.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:23.850
So the forgetting will
be kind of disabled.

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:25.383
So this is one gate,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.160
the other gate is the input gate.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:34.080
So the input gate controls
how much information flows

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.823
from the input of X.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:40.710
So for example, in the same sentence,

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:43.500
if you talk about Bob here and Bob there

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.070
so there are some new
contexts describing Bob.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:50.760
You want to have this new information

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:53.370
being kind of merged or fused

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:58.230
into the original like context vector.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.603
And this is called like in the input gate.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:05.940
And once you have like the
input and the forget gate,

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.980
and then you have the context.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.170
So from this context,

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.770
this context is presumably multifaceted

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:18.330
because it captures like a
lot of aspects about Bob.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:22.080
But like right here, if we
want to generate the next word,

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:26.910
so it's reflecting a
certain aspect of Bob.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.700
So that's why we need some to choose

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:31.480
or select some aspect out of

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:34.650
the hidden states that we have,

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:36.540
and that's why we have the output gate.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.700
So the output gate will be like more like

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:42.320
a selective module trying to only select

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:47.880
the most relevant information
out of the context.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:52.470
So these are like the three
big modules inside the LSTM

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:55.770
to capture different sorts of behaviors.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:59.070
Because in your sentence
or in your paragraph

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:01.350
sometimes you need to forget,

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.450
and you need to start from scratch,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:06.240
and sometimes you need
to digest the information

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.950
and sometimes you need to select

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:10.500
which information to output.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:14.463
So this is the underlying
mechanism of LSTM.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:20.630
So for LSTM it can be also
used for machine translation.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.750
So the basic idea is that
for a lot of these words,

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.060
we can use word embedding

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.990
and each word can be
represented as a vector.

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:34.893
So these vectors will be
basically the X from here,

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.670
and you have this X

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.610
and then you run through
the LSTM mechanism

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.380
that I talked about.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.860
Try to process this X
one by one and try to get

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.243
the context vector of this LSTM.

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:54.720
And then you have this context fracture,

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.820
and then you have another LSTM,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:59.110
which is the decoder that tries to

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.630
condition on the context
and trying to output

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:05.430
the words token by token.

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:10.110
It's basically, there are a
lot of optimizable parameters

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:13.410
both of this LSTM, so you can optimize it

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.200
to generate the things that you want.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.400
Try to kind of twist it

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.163
to do the things that you want basically.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:25.230
So here, like this is an example of

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:28.080
a neuro machine
translation using our LSTM.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.213
So you fit this word by word,

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:34.710
and then you use the last
hidden stage of the LSTM,

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.170
and then you fit it into the decoder,

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:40.290
and then the decoder will
also go through this LSTM

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:42.783
and try to generate a token by token.

00:12:47.015 --> 00:12:50.970
And then we're going to move
on to the attention mechanism.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:55.860
So one of the problem
of the previous approach

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:59.160
is that we only, and
there are two problems.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:02.160
One is that we are basically processing

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:03.993
the input like token by token.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.710
For example, if there are two tokens,

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:10.710
that I position and J position,

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:15.660
you literally need to run the
model, like J minus I steps,

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:20.640
to let these two tokens
interact with each other.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.760
So this is very common
in natural language.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.830
We have a lot of co reference.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.030
This course structure,

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:30.120
we keep referring to things
that we talked about.

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:35.120
But sometimes these words
can be separated far apart.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:40.110
And so these kind of like
token by token architecture

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:44.040
cannot capture this kind of
long dependency very well.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:48.450
And secondly, as you can
see in the last video,

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.630
so the model basically
compress all the information

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.450
from the encoder side into one vector.

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:55.950
Sometimes this vector,

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:58.620
you know, this vector
is a fixed size vector.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:01.710
For example, you feel like
have like a whole paragraph.

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.300
It's impossible to compress

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.703
all of them into just one vector.

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:10.110
Yeah. So how can we make
this model more flexible.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.210
Is there an easy fix?

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:14.820
So in this part I'll mainly talk about

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:17.280
how we address the second issue.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.853
How to make the context
vector more flexible.

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:27.690
Which is easy, basically
previously we only passed

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:30.930
one vector from the
encoder to the decoder.

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:33.660
So maybe we can just fit all the vectors

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.310
from the encoder to the decoder

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:37.200
This is a very easy fix, right?

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.760
So you goes through the encoder RNN

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:43.590
and you get first hidden
state, and then the second,

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:46.530
and then the third you
just compile them together

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.140
and then feed all of them to the decoder,

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.960
and then the decoder will be able to,

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.620
like be able to figure out what to grab

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:59.100
from the encoder side instead
of like the encoder to decide

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.410
what should I compress, right?

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:05.400
So that's what we call
as attention mechanism

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.680
like from the decoder side.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.900
So the attention decoder

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.620
doesn't actually stop
before producing output.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.670
It basically looks at the
set of encoder hidden states.

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:21.840
So each encoder hidden
state is mostly associated

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:25.080
with certain words because it's produced

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:26.283
like token by token.

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:28.320
And we're going to...

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:33.090
So the decoder will give each
of the hidden state a score,

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:38.090
basically like how relevant
this state is with respect to

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:41.190
the one things that I want to output.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.710
And then we're going to
multiply each hidden state

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:47.940
by its soft max score and thus amplifying

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.000
the hidden state that are
relevant to my output,

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:54.000
and then throwing away the hidden states

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:57.393
that are irrelevant to
what I want to pursue.

00:15:58.388 --> 00:16:00.510
So in order to better understand this,

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:01.920
you can see this video.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.603
So basically you have,

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:07.440
let's say here you have
some encoded hidden state

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:10.770
like H1, H2, H3, you know,

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:13.470
for example, this is a
very simple visualization.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:15.990
Let's say we only have three words.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:18.360
And then we want to
decode the hidden state

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:21.180
for the times step four.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:24.250
And then now we kind of
attend to all of this

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:28.140
encoder hidden state, and then we got to,

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.580
we get their score, right?

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:32.730
So basically how relevant they are.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.010
But this is not normalized.

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:36.700
We can use some soft max

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:42.060
like a normalization to
get a distribution of this.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:45.540
And then you're going to
multiply the softmax score

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:47.520
with all this hidden state

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:50.580
and then aggregate them
into one vector, right?

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.890
So this vector will represent

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:57.170
what I need from the encoder output.

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.520
So this is pretty dynamic

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.210
and it's doing very
well in a lot of cases.

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:04.710
So the attention flow,

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:08.130
like more formally can
be expressed as this,

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:12.630
so the RNN processes input

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:13.830
producing an output,

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:16.647
and then a new hidden state vector, H4.

00:17:16.647 --> 00:17:20.400
And so because now it's at
the fourth stack, right?

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:21.930
So in the attention step,

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:24.990
we're going to use the
encoder hidden states

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:29.906
and the H4 vector to calculate
the context of vector

00:17:29.906 --> 00:17:34.260
for this step, basically using
the mechanism of weighted sum

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:35.820
that I just mentioned,

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:40.290
and then we concatenate
H4 and C4 into one vector,

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:42.690
and this vector will be fed into

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:44.910
another feedforward neural network.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:48.513
And this neural network will
decide which word to output.

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:54.450
So this is basically the
fourth step computation.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:58.113
We can repeat this for
any number of times.

00:17:59.899 --> 00:18:03.660
So here is another video of
teaching you how to do this.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.363
So here we are at the fourth step,

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:12.420
because we already have like
three encoder hidden state

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:14.580
and we are generating the fourth one.

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:18.926
So we will fit like the start token here,

00:18:18.926 --> 00:18:21.090
and then we will have like the H4

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:24.510
it's like some initialized vector.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:29.510
And then I will use this to
query all these hidden states.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:33.060
So there are one, two,
three, four of them,

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:35.580
and then you calculate the softmax score

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:38.670
and then you do the weighted sum,

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:43.230
and then you get this
hidden stage, which is C4.

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:47.130
And then you concatenate
C4 and H4 together

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:50.430
and then put that into a
feedforward neural network

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:52.203
and then it will generate the work.

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:55.140
Hopefully this is I, right?

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:58.320
And then on this I will be like,

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:01.260
this embedding will
fit into the step five.

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:04.353
And then you just go through
like the whole thing again.

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:09.180
And then it'll attend to, you know,

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:13.860
all this in hidden state,
encoded hidden states again.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:15.660
Now it's attending to a different thing

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:19.440
because when you are
translating your position moves,

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:22.050
you attend to different context

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:25.230
depending on the position of your decoder,

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:28.260
and then you fit it to maybe like a,

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:31.140
to get C5 feedforward neural network,

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:33.780
and then you generate
the output of M, right?

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:34.740
So on and so forth.

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:36.790
You can run this until

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:41.193
like end of sentence token or whatsoever.

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:46.920
And one thing that we observe is that

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.860
if you plot out the attention map,

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:54.090
like attention map basically
is like the softmax score.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:57.630
So let's say this is
like the decoder side,

00:19:57.630 --> 00:19:59.940
and this is the encoder side.

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:02.580
So when I'm translating the first word,

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:06.930
it lays a lot of attention on this word,

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:08.280
like this French word.

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:09.750
When I'm translating this,

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:13.140
the attention kind of highlights on this,

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:14.100
so on and so forth.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:16.320
You can see there's like the overarching

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:18.150
like diagonal trend.

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.910
So if you know French and English

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:25.910
you can see that these token
from English and French,

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:27.450
they're aligned very well.

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:32.450
So which also enhances the
interpretability of the model.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:39.320
And even more we could generalize
this attention based LSTM

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:42.690
to do image captioning as well.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:46.350
So what we're going to do is
that instead of like using RNNs

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:49.050
to process word as the input,

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:52.680
we can use CNNs to process
image as the input.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:56.190
Now we have like the CNN going to give us

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:57.600
like a feature map, right?

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:01.657
So you can flatten this
feature map just like the RNN,

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:06.090
so you will have a lot
of localized features

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:08.880
extracted from the input image,

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:12.030
and then you use the LSTM
with some attention mechanism

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:16.650
and then try to generate
the caption word by word.

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:19.530
And as you can see, some of the people,

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:24.530
I think this was
published at Mila in 2015,

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:28.230
this is a very, a highly cited paper.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:30.480
So if you are going to
visualize the attention,

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.079
so we can see like

00:21:32.079 --> 00:21:35.190
when the captioning
model is generating bird,

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:38.850
it tries to attend to
bird most of the time.

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:42.450
When it's generating like
something like water or something,

00:21:42.450 --> 00:21:46.650
it blocks out the bird

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:49.200
will attend more to the surrounding water

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.670
or something like that.

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:52.800
So this is very fancy at that time

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:55.860
people have never seen
such kind of mechanism.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:59.010
So that's why it arouses a lot of interest

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:02.223
in pursuing this type of, you know,

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:06.003
interpretable generative model.

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:10.260
And then that's why we
have the transformer model.

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:15.260
So I already talked about
like how to kind of break the,

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.790
let me see what's the time?

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:21.450
So it's 1:00, it's already 1:00.

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:24.810
Yeah, it's to 2:30, right?

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:26.223
Okay, sounds good.

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:30.660
We already talk about how
we break the bottleneck

00:22:30.660 --> 00:22:32.970
of the contact vector,

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:36.090
so we haven't touched on
upon the other weakness,

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:41.090
which is how can we capture
the long-term dependency

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:46.950
between tokens that are
further apart in the input,

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:49.830
and how can we make it more flexible?

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:53.490
If you look at the recurrent
neural network architecture,

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.070
if you unroll it from left to right,

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:59.550
you can see that something
like this, right?

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:03.300
And the encoder, so the RNNs

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:07.110
and tries to use the injective buyers that

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:08.640
like all the sequences,

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.010
a lot of the contacts are local, right?

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:13.530
They try to process things locally,

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:15.660
which is a good heuristic,

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:17.730
but it also has some bottleneck.

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:20.640
For example, the RNNs going to take

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:23.460
the order of sequence length time

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:28.140
to make the distant word
interact with each other, right?

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:32.580
So for example, if you are
processing this sentence

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:36.030
the chef who blah, blah,
blah, blah and worse.

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.670
And then this worse and chef, they are

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.270
they're highly connected, right?

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:44.151
But there might be, you know,

00:23:44.151 --> 00:23:45.750
I mean a hundred tokens in between.

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:48.930
So the model already forget
what's going on with the chef,

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:52.560
you know, maybe mis remembers
that it's not a chef,

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.200
it's maybe a doctor or maybe a policeman

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:56.580
or something like that.

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:59.304
So this could cause a lot of issues

00:23:59.304 --> 00:24:02.160
when we're doing NLP, right?

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:07.160
So with this like kind
of linearized recurrence,

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:13.020
it's very hard for us to learn
the long distance dependency.

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:16.380
And this is mainly because
the gradient propagation issue

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:19.830
because it's using the BPTT algorithm

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:27.240
and you basically need
to propagate the gradient

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:30.510
by many steps from here to here

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:32.880
to actually learn the
long-term dependency.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:36.720
So this learning paradigm
basically prevents the model

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:40.710
from like getting, capturing
this long-term dependency.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:45.457
And so this is not the
best way to do an NLP.

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:52.440
So that's why like I think
in 2017 or something,

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.110
so Google has published this paper called

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:57.520
Attention Is All you Need and basically

00:24:59.509 --> 00:25:03.480
proposed that we don't even
need the recurrent architecture,

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:06.720
we can just use attention
for the whole network.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:10.320
The whole network is just
built upon attention mechanism.

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:11.880
How can we do that, right?

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:15.660
So we going to use like the...

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:20.370
Attention going to treat each
word's representation as a query,

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:24.480
and then trying to access
and incorporate information

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:26.700
from the entire sequence, right?

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:29.520
So there is no recurrence at all here.

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:33.030
And one good thing about
this is it can be done

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:36.510
in a very highly parallelized way.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:39.720
Because in GPU it has a lot of this

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:42.030
parallelized computation unit.

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:45.330
If you use transformer,
it can best exploit

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:49.027
the parallelism residing inside the GPU.

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:55.650
So right now, because there
is no kind of recurrence,

00:25:55.650 --> 00:26:00.270
the maximum interaction
distance or the complexity

00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:02.820
for two tokens to interact with each other

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:06.180
suddenly drop to constant
or one basically,

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:08.640
and all the words will interact

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:11.100
with each other at all level.

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:14.550
So for example, here in the second layer,

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:18.450
so this hidden stage
going to interact with

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:21.060
all the input word embedding.

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:24.300
And then you're going to
do this self attention

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:25.800
over this whole layer.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.650
And then you're going to stack
another self attention layer

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:31.800
on top of that, and then you on this layer

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:36.090
you can also kind of interact
with all the hidden states

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:39.690
from the last layer, so on and so forth.

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:43.210
You can stack this maybe a hundred layers

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:48.060
to capture all sorts of very
sophisticated interactions

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:51.690
between different words
and so on and so forth.

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:56.490
So attention can be seen as a
performing a very fuzzy lookup

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:58.530
in a key value store.

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:03.530
So in a lookup table, we have
a table of key and values.

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:06.303
Basically if here we are having a word,

00:27:06.303 --> 00:27:09.750
lets query, and then we try to grab

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:12.300
the most relevant words out of this

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:14.730
inventory of key and value pairs,

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:17.310
and then we're going to do the output.

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:19.140
But if you're going to design it this way,

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:23.070
like some hard selection,
this is not differentiable.

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:24.810
So that's why they do like

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:28.590
a more softer version of like lookout.

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:31.170
So this is also based on
the attention mechanism

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:32.880
that I already talked about.

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:35.580
You just compute like the score

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:38.640
with respect to each of the key value pair

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:42.060
and do a softmax over all of them,

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:45.093
and then do the weighted sum
to calculate all of them.

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:48.660
But in this case, most
of the time the softmax

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:52.980
try to polarize the attention weights.

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:55.920
And then if you look at the attention,

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:57.870
it's mostly specified.

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:01.170
And then the model is
only kind of attending to

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:04.653
several individual key value pairs.

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:08.430
So this is like the transformer encoder,

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:09.960
which consists of two part.

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:12.990
One is the self attention
layer that I talked about

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:15.930
where like the model
kind of attend to itself

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:20.730
and then it's all of this nearby
tokens within the sequence.

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:22.850
And then after that we
will have like another

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:26.400
feetforward neural network trying to add

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:31.260
more express like power into it,

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:33.420
like adding more non-linearity here,

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:35.490
so on and so forth, right?

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:38.730
So here, if we have only have two words

00:28:38.730 --> 00:28:42.840
we can help design like
the self attention layer

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:47.310
and then it'll let this two
tokens interact with each other

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:50.250
to get the representation of Z1, Z2.

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:52.230
And then you're going to put that through

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:54.010
like a feetforward neural network

00:28:55.937 --> 00:29:00.180
to calculate the real
output of this single block.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:03.630
And then you can do this
like layer by layer.

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:06.600
So for example, if you
have N encoder layers,

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:08.550
you're going to repeat this process

00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:13.550
until you finally reach the last layer.

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:16.110
So here is basically representing,

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:18.390
like repeating what I said.

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:20.400
Basically if you have two words,

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:25.400
you're going to project the
word embedding into three,

00:29:25.547 --> 00:29:28.380
the three different vectors,

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:29.580
one's the query vector,

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:33.840
one's a key vector and one
is the value vector, right?

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:38.100
And then you're going
to use the query and key

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:39.253
to calculate one score.

00:29:39.253 --> 00:29:42.000
So this is basically dark product

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:46.140
and then you can see you have
like this two dark product

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:49.230
and then you normalize
it into the softmax,

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:53.820
and then you combine them
with the weighted sum

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:55.620
and then you will get Z one.

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:59.670
So basically the think will
find it after the self attention

00:29:59.670 --> 00:30:02.010
will also generate like one vector.

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.690
And similarly for machine,

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:05.220
you will do the same thing

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.170
and then you will generate another vector.

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:09.480
And like in a nutshell,

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:13.683
this can be expressed in
a matrix format like this.

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:19.939
And like another very smart design

00:30:19.939 --> 00:30:24.630
in this transformer is that
it not only has one attention,

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:27.540
it has multiple attention,
parallel attention,

00:30:27.540 --> 00:30:29.490
which we call multi-head, right?

00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:33.071
Multi-head attention it
increases the model's capacity

00:30:33.071 --> 00:30:35.040
to attempt differently.

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:36.990
So basically like in this word,

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:40.140
it probably like in this
aspect, it tend to this word.

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:42.240
Maybe it's trying to express something,

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:43.560
attend to some other words.

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:45.030
So there are different,

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:49.230
like this could increase
the model's capacity

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:52.653
to leverage different
attention from the input.

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:56.730
And what it does is basically

00:30:56.730 --> 00:31:00.240
you have like this multi
attention output, right?

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:02.550
You just concatenate all of them together

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:05.670
and then you go through
another projection layer

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:09.693
to finally combine them all
this multi-head into one output.

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:14.610
And yeah, so there are some
layer normalization after this.

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:16.770
I'll just fast forward this part

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:21.270
and then you can stack a lot
of this self attention layers

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:22.830
on top of each other.

00:31:22.830 --> 00:31:25.773
And on the decoder part,
it's also self attention.

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:29.910
But there is another caveat.

00:31:29.910 --> 00:31:31.950
It's not only attending to itself,

00:31:31.950 --> 00:31:35.220
but it's also attending to
the encoder output, right?

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:38.010
So here you're going to
switch out how you calculate

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:41.520
the key and value on pairs.

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:42.870
It's not coming from itself,

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:44.970
it's basically coming from the ones

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:47.763
that you already
calculated by the encoder.

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:51.690
And then we're going to talk
about self supervised learning.

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:54.660
So transformer is basically
the most important

00:31:54.660 --> 00:31:56.310
architecture right now.

00:31:56.310 --> 00:32:00.063
So a lot of the work has been
done on top of transformers.

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:03.900
And so here we've just
assumed that we have

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:05.910
like a transformer model.

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:10.910
So like one of the key like task

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:12.960
in natural language processing,

00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:14.640
it's called language model.

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:17.370
Language modeling is
basically trying to predict

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:20.340
the next word given the
past context, right?

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:25.340
And so like, and actually
in order to learn this,

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:27.600
you don't actually need any annotation

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:30.600
or any supervised data
because it's basically

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:33.720
just you grab some contacts
from the web, right?

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:36.600
And then you can have some past contacts

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:39.900
and to predict the next
token and so on and so forth.

00:32:39.900 --> 00:32:42.840
This is what we call a
self supervision, right?

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:45.360
So it's not supervised by any human

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:47.730
or anything or more explicitly.

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:52.530
So, and it's trying to get
the signal from the web.

00:32:52.530 --> 00:32:57.530
And try to infuse this
into the neural parameters.

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:01.110
And then once we train this model

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:03.120
to perform this language modeling,

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:06.810
we can save this pre-trained
model as the checkpoint.

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:09.810
And then once we do some downstream tasks,

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:11.790
we're going to initialize our weights

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:14.790
from the save the
checkpoint so that we could

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:18.180
do the optimization more efficiently.

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:20.860
So for the self supervised learning,

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:23.850
there were a lot of stuff
going on at that time.

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:28.850
I think I remember it in
like 2018, 2019 or 2020.

00:33:29.100 --> 00:33:31.770
There are different studies
trying to investigate

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:35.280
what is the best self
supervised objective.

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:39.420
So like the OpenAI trust
is next token prediction.

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:42.510
They have been always
doing this even in GPT-3.

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:44.973
So the basic idea is very simple.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:50.220
You have a sentence you
found from like the web,

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:53.490
and then you mask the next
word like mask some word

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:55.800
and then use the past window,

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:58.830
the previous window and
then try to predict that,

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:01.260
that's called next token prediction.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:03.450
And then also some other work like BERT

00:34:03.450 --> 00:34:05.940
who tries to explore other alternative

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:08.040
where you have like a whole sentence

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:10.080
and then you mask one of the token, right?

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:12.030
And then you try to predict this word,

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:13.500
not only from the left hand side,

00:34:13.500 --> 00:34:15.200
but also from the right hand side.

00:34:17.529 --> 00:34:20.760
And there are also other
objective for example,

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:22.560
if you have a complete sentence

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:24.420
and then you have another,

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:26.700
some other sentence you're
going to predict which one

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:29.670
is the sentence that
follows this sentence.

00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:33.180
Or there are also other
sentence reordering task

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:38.180
where you kind of perturb all
the words inside the sentence

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:40.140
and then you try to reconstruct

00:34:40.140 --> 00:34:41.940
the original ordering.

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:45.570
So there are tons of papers
in investigating this

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:48.780
like GPT-2, BERT, RoBERTa, Ernie, T5,

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:52.140
Albert, Bart, UL2, so on and so forth.

00:34:52.140 --> 00:34:54.000
There are plenty of them.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:56.610
And so some of them are using like

00:34:56.610 --> 00:35:00.030
decoder only architecture where basically

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:02.370
like the whole architecture
just a decoder.

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:04.320
There's no encoder at all.

00:35:04.320 --> 00:35:07.374
Some examples are like GPT-2, GPT-3,

00:35:07.374 --> 00:35:11.490
LaMDA and there are some
only using the encoder

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:13.890
and there's no decoder like BERT

00:35:13.890 --> 00:35:16.893
and some other variants
like RoBERTa whatsoever.

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:22.020
And there's also like
encoder decoder architecture

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:26.013
like transformers, T5,
BERT, so on and so forth.

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:29.460
And so now like with all this

00:35:29.460 --> 00:35:31.470
super self supervised learning,

00:35:31.470 --> 00:35:34.470
so the community shifted
to another paradigm

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:36.600
where, well, whatever we do,

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:38.700
we first try to pre-train the model

00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:40.860
on some self supervised tasks

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:44.610
and then we fine tune on our end task

00:35:44.610 --> 00:35:48.330
like machine translation
dialogue, anything, right?

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:52.800
So this has bumped the score up by a lot.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:57.000
So if you look at like the
fine tune transformer model,

00:35:57.000 --> 00:35:59.550
so like for example, like the GPT-2,

00:35:59.550 --> 00:36:02.040
it's able to achieve so high performance

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:05.790
on a lot of the benchmarks.

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:08.370
Like here, these are like some,

00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:10.830
I think it's natural
language inference task

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:13.770
and then also some reading
comprehension task.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:17.010
So people have observed
that with this pre-training,

00:36:17.010 --> 00:36:20.340
the end model performance can jump a lot

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:22.203
in a lot of the cases.

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:25.230
And then finally we're going to talk about

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:26.880
a large language model.

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:30.450
So large language model
has been very popular.

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:33.230
So this is before GPT-3.

00:36:33.230 --> 00:36:37.410
As you can see, ELMo is also
like a pre-training master,

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:39.840
which was proposed in 2017,

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:42.750
which only uses 94 million parameters.

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:46.470
And then we have like GPT, BERT,

00:36:46.470 --> 00:36:49.770
transformer ELMo, GPT-2,
so on and so forth.

00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:52.710
Most of them are only
residing in the regime

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:56.400
of like several hundreds
of millions of parameters

00:36:56.400 --> 00:36:57.960
except the Nvidia one,

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:00.180
which is the megaelectron-LM,

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:03.723
which has 8 billion parameters.

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:07.560
And then GPT-3 come out, right?

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:10.560
You can see that all these models

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:14.910
they compare so small,
like with respect to GPT-3.

00:37:14.910 --> 00:37:18.750
GPT-3 just increases from
like hundreds of million

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:20.160
to hundreds of billion.

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:21.930
You know, it's a thousand times larger

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:23.913
than all the other models.

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:25.950
So it's crazy.

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:30.750
Like one of the key
observation from OpenAI is that

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:32.190
it's called scaling law.

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:34.320
Scaling law means that if you train

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:38.790
this large language model,
and then you observe

00:37:38.790 --> 00:37:42.540
it's final like converged test loss,

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:44.820
test loss is basically a perplexity.

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:48.240
It's basically telling
you how perplexed you are

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:50.610
in terms of generating the next token

00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:54.510
on a validation coppers
or something like that.

00:37:54.510 --> 00:37:57.930
So presumably the lower tasks loss

00:37:57.930 --> 00:37:59.940
means that the model is better.

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:03.060
It correlates very well
with the downstream task.

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:04.413
And as you can see,

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:08.010
so this is basically a linear line.

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:11.320
It means that the OpenAI found that

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:15.240
the more compute you throw in,

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:16.920
the better model you can get.

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:21.270
And then it also scales log-linearly.

00:38:21.270 --> 00:38:24.540
If you use the empirical results

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:29.070
and then you try to fit
it, it's almost one line.

00:38:29.070 --> 00:38:30.420
It's almost a line, right?

00:38:30.420 --> 00:38:35.040
So with this, you can almost
deterministically predict

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:38.583
how well your model is
biospecific compute.

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:42.870
And it's also logged linearly
with respect to the token,

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:45.510
the dataset size that you are training at,

00:38:45.510 --> 00:38:49.830
like basically how many tokens
you have in your dataset.

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:53.820
So here, there are scales
from like a few million to,

00:38:53.820 --> 00:38:55.860
I think this is like a billion, right?

00:38:55.860 --> 00:38:58.530
So right now the models
that we are training

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:01.980
are using like hundreds
of billions of tokens.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:04.860
And then it's also lot-linearly scales

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:07.863
with the parameter size of the model.

00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:14.040
And you can see that GPT-3
tries to investigate it.

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:18.480
What would be the outcome if
we train a lot of these models.

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:21.810
From like small, medium, large excel,

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:25.230
so on and so forth into 175 billion,

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:26.760
which is GPT-3 right?

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:29.340
So we can see that all these models,

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:32.880
they fit this like their lowest point

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:36.240
in the converging test validation loss,

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:38.190
it fits this line very well.

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:40.350
This is a huge discovery.

00:39:40.350 --> 00:39:43.020
It basically give us a guide of how well

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:45.900
I can actually get with when I throw in

00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:48.250
this amount of computer
or something like that.

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:53.220
And then with these models now,

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:56.760
like open AI found like a
new way to use this model.

00:39:56.760 --> 00:40:00.510
Basically, you don't need to
kind of fine tune it at all.

00:40:00.510 --> 00:40:01.620
You just prompt it

00:40:01.620 --> 00:40:04.260
and then it will generate
things that you want.

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:07.380
So there are different
prompting methodology,

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:08.640
there's no prompt one,

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:11.910
which means that there's no
natural language instruction.

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:14.160
You just give it some examples.

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:17.850
Like there are zero
shot, one shot few shots.

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:21.300
And then there's ones with
natural language instruction,

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:23.790
basically telling the
model what you are doing

00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:27.300
and also giving you some
sort of demonstration

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:29.283
about how this task is being solved.

00:40:30.360 --> 00:40:33.090
And then the in-context
learning, you know,

00:40:33.090 --> 00:40:34.470
basically when you are doing

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:36.420
gradient descent over the model,

00:40:36.420 --> 00:40:40.053
it implicitly learns how to
do the in-context learning.

00:40:41.370 --> 00:40:42.897
And so what this model like,

00:40:42.897 --> 00:40:45.360
and so here are some
of the examples, right?

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:49.053
So you basically having
some sort of demonstration,

00:40:49.053 --> 00:40:53.490
then the model kind of imitate
the behavior very well.

00:40:53.490 --> 00:40:57.697
And so the in-content learning
is defined as following.

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:03.270
So the model develops a
pattern recognition abilities

00:41:03.270 --> 00:41:06.390
while training, which can be
applied to inference time.

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:09.390
So the capability is emergent

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:11.790
as the model scales up.

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:14.277
Which means that when the model is small,

00:41:14.277 --> 00:41:18.630
like the model can't do this
in contextual anywhere well.

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:22.020
But if you scale it up to like
a hundred billion parameters,

00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:24.120
it suddenly learns how to do it

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:27.540
and do it very well basically.

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:30.450
And so, however, if you look into

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:32.850
the unsupervised learning objective,

00:41:32.850 --> 00:41:36.480
there's no explicit
guidance to teach the model

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:37.920
how to do in-context learning,

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:40.590
how to imitate a few demonstration

00:41:40.590 --> 00:41:45.510
and then do the like same
thing on the unseen input.

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:48.210
But there are a lot of
active ongoing research

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:52.113
trying to investigate why, how
this kind of behavior emerge.

00:41:53.580 --> 00:41:57.450
And so these are some of the like results

00:41:57.450 --> 00:41:59.040
provided by OpenAI.

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:02.730
So during the training
uses some very exact task

00:42:02.730 --> 00:42:06.387
like remove a random symbol from a word.

00:42:06.387 --> 00:42:08.400
As you can see,

00:42:08.400 --> 00:42:11.130
by increasing the demonstration examples,

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:14.940
the model tries to like
gets much and much better

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:17.070
performance and as you can see,

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:19.410
the larger the model gets

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:22.980
the performance it will
increase dramatically

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:27.030
from like 20% to 60%, something like that.

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:30.420
And one thing that we also observe is that

00:42:30.420 --> 00:42:32.640
when you have enough examples,

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:35.913
you don't even need the
natural language instruction.

00:42:38.513 --> 00:42:40.770
You just give it some like examples.

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:43.650
The model can already
reach the same performance

00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:46.503
with the counterpart with
natural language instruction.

00:42:47.855 --> 00:42:52.188
(audience member speaking faintly)

00:42:54.510 --> 00:42:56.010
Could you repeat the question?

00:42:56.850 --> 00:42:58.380
Oh yeah, I'm sorry.

00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:00.630
So you were saying that,

00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:03.453
how does the model evaluate this, right?

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:12.810
Measure? So they basically
use a synthetic task.

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:17.100
So basically requires the
model to remove a random symbol

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:20.940
from an input sequence like A, B, C, D.

00:43:20.940 --> 00:43:24.360
Tries to remove any
maybe C or D or anything

00:43:24.360 --> 00:43:26.130
and then generate like the output.

00:43:26.130 --> 00:43:28.830
So this is a very easy
task just trying to do

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:31.770
some diagnostic about like whether model

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:33.843
can learn this very simple skill.

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:44.583
The parameters, learnable
parameters from the model.

00:43:45.750 --> 00:43:50.750
Yeah. So there are like
1.6, 1.3 billion parameter,

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:56.090
13 billion parameter and
175 billion parameters.

00:43:56.190 --> 00:43:59.070
As you can see, you know,
as you scale up the model,

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:02.343
the performance going to
jump very significantly.

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:06.060
And so that's like some
synthetic test, right?

00:44:06.060 --> 00:44:10.440
Let's look at some more
realistic task called TriviaQA.

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:13.140
So this comprises of a lot of questions

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:16.140
from this TriviaQA test

00:44:16.140 --> 00:44:18.720
and it's asking about
like some random knowledge

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:20.030
in our daily life, right?

00:44:20.030 --> 00:44:23.130
So you can see that as
the model scales up,

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:26.250
the performance can
improve very significantly,

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:30.900
like from maybe like 10 to 30 to 50 until

00:44:30.900 --> 00:44:32.580
like 70 or something.

00:44:32.580 --> 00:44:35.220
And what surprises us is that

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:36.870
the model hasn't converged yet.

00:44:36.870 --> 00:44:41.220
It's kind of in like
improving linearly right now.

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:43.020
So which means that if you scale the model

00:44:43.020 --> 00:44:45.180
to like a trillion parameter or something,

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:48.480
maybe you can already get
a hundred percent accuracy.

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:50.973
So this is very intriguing.

00:44:58.020 --> 00:45:01.530
Basically you give the model

00:45:01.530 --> 00:45:05.853
like some sort of examples
to imitate basically.

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:13.410
Yeah. That's why like the
improvement is not very dramatic

00:45:13.410 --> 00:45:16.620
Just a few percentage
of if you look at that.

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:19.173
So it's only like a few percentage.

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:22.560
But you at least need like one example

00:45:22.560 --> 00:45:23.850
to actually teach the model

00:45:23.850 --> 00:45:27.570
what is the format of the
answer something like that.

00:45:27.570 --> 00:45:28.590
But giving it, you know,

00:45:28.590 --> 00:45:31.170
like 64, 63 more, you know,

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:33.000
you only get a very small jump

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:36.393
like maybe seven or 5%
something like that.

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:40.320
And then the GPT-3 if you
are going to evaluate it

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:45.320
on some of the major NLP
task like machine translation

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:50.250
and whatsoever like reading comprehension.

00:45:50.250 --> 00:45:51.780
Without training the model at all,

00:45:51.780 --> 00:45:54.000
you just give it like a few shot example

00:45:54.000 --> 00:45:55.380
and then do prompting,

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:58.320
the model is already getting
a very good performance

00:45:58.320 --> 00:45:59.910
on a lot of these tasks,

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:02.760
and these results are coming from 2020.

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:04.660
So these are pretty old right now

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:08.430
I assume that with GPT-4
you already get like

00:46:08.430 --> 00:46:10.203
much, much higher performance.

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:15.660
So why is in-context
learning so good, right?

00:46:15.660 --> 00:46:20.070
Why does people love
in-context learning so much?

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:23.100
I guess one reason being
that the previous master

00:46:23.100 --> 00:46:26.640
still requires the model
you to fine tune the model.

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:29.610
But fine tuning is very expensive.

00:46:29.610 --> 00:46:31.650
The expense comes from two sides.

00:46:31.650 --> 00:46:34.320
One is that if you want
to do gradient descent,

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:36.870
you need a lot of GPUs, right?

00:46:36.870 --> 00:46:38.790
GPU memory whatsoever.

00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:40.210
And then you also need to,

00:46:40.210 --> 00:46:42.120
after fine tuning the model,

00:46:42.120 --> 00:46:44.850
you need to save the
checkpoint in your disk.

00:46:44.850 --> 00:46:46.650
Like each of the checkpoint
could be, you know,

00:46:46.650 --> 00:46:51.270
like tens of gigabytes which
is very expensive to save.

00:46:51.270 --> 00:46:53.280
But now with in-context learning,

00:46:53.280 --> 00:46:56.160
you don't need to do any
gradient design at all.

00:46:56.160 --> 00:46:58.920
You just give it some prompt

00:46:58.920 --> 00:47:00.660
and then it does everything for you.

00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:03.390
And then one single model
can solve all the tasks

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:05.790
without any adaptation.

00:47:05.790 --> 00:47:07.290
The adaptation is only on the input,

00:47:07.290 --> 00:47:10.890
so what kind of input you try to give it,

00:47:10.890 --> 00:47:12.780
what kind of demonstration you give it.

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:14.940
That's why problem engineering becomes

00:47:14.940 --> 00:47:17.253
like a very popular fashion right now.

00:47:18.600 --> 00:47:21.663
And yeah, so with that I
want to conclude this talk.

